                        "question": "3a. How useful are Sources C and D for an enquiry into the challenges facing the early Weimar Republic? Explain your answer, using Sources C and D and your knowledge of the historical context. (8 marks)",
                        "model": "<p>Source C is highly useful because its content reveals the extreme physical suffering of the German population, observing that they \"are crying out for bread.\" Using contextual knowledge, the Allied Naval Blockade caused mass starvation, which directly fueled radical uprisings like the Spartacist revolt in January 1919. The provenance of Source C—a diary entry by an Englishwoman living in Germany—makes it highly useful as an uncensored, firsthand account of the desperate mood on the home front.</p><p>Source D is incredibly useful for showing the political instability of the new government. Its content shows that Chancellor Ebert was forced into a desperate alliance with the old, conservative Imperial Army, promising to \"fight against Bolshevism.\" Contextually, this refers to the Ebert-Groener Pact, proving the Republic had no democratic military of its own and was entirely dependent on right-wing forces to survive. The provenance of Source D—a memoir from General Groener himself—makes it extremely useful because it provides insider confirmation of this secret, undemocratic deal that kept the Republic alive but fundamentally compromised its democratic foundations.</p>"
