                        "question": "3a. How useful are Sources C and D for an enquiry into the extent of German economic recovery in the years 1924-29? (8 marks)",
                        "model": "<p>Source C is highly useful for an enquiry into the extent of the economic recovery because it highlights the underlying fragility of the Weimar economy. The content explicitly states that Germany is \"dancing on a volcano\" and relies heavily on \"short-term loans from America.\" This is accurate to my contextual knowledge, as the Dawes Plan (1924) flooded Germany with US capital that could be recalled at any time. The provenance of the source makes it particularly valuable: it is a speech by Gustav Stresemann, the architect of the recovery, given privately to his own party in 1929. Because he is speaking to his political allies rather than the general public, he is likely being remarkably frank and honest about the structural weaknesses of the economy, making it a highly reliable piece of evidence for the 'quicksand' nature of the recovery.</p><p>Source D is also useful, but it provides a contrasting perspective by illustrating the visible, surface-level prosperity of the era. The photograph shows a bustling Berlin street with \"well-dressed shoppers,\" \"modern motor cars,\" and a \"newly built department store.\" This accurately reflects the rising living standards, consumerism, and cultural boom experienced in urban centers during the 'Golden Age' of 1924-29, where real wages increased by 10% by 1928. However, its usefulness is limited by its nature as a published photograph in an illustrated magazine. It was likely selected to showcase modernity and wealth, thereby ignoring the less glamorous realities of the period, such as the agricultural depression where farmers faced plummeting incomes, or the persistent unemployment that never fell below 1.3 million. Together, however, both sources are highly useful as they perfectly capture the dual nature of the Weimar recovery: outward prosperity built on a precarious foundation.</p>"
