                    {
                        "question": "3a. How useful are Sources C and D for an enquiry into how Hitler became Chancellor in 1933? (8 marks)",
                        "model": "<p>Source C is highly useful for an enquiry into how Hitler became Chancellor because it provides direct insight into the mindset of the conservative elites who orchestrated his appointment. The content reveals von Papen's supreme arrogance, explicitly stating \"we have hired him\" and predicting Hitler would be pushed into a corner until he \"squeaks\". This is useful as it demonstrates the elite miscalculation that Hitler could be easily controlled. The provenance enhances its utility; as a statement from von Papen himself in January 1933, it reflects the exact justifications he used to persuade President Hindenburg to agree to the coalition. Contextually, this aligns with the historical reality that Hitler's cabinet initially contained only two other Nazis (Frick and Göring), explaining why Papen felt so confident that the conservatives had Hitler \"framed in\".</p><p>Source D is also very useful as it illustrates contemporary left-wing perspectives on Hitler's rise to power. The visual content, showing Hitler as a marionette puppet manipulated by von Papen and industrialists, is useful because it highlights the widespread belief that Hitler was merely a tool of the traditional conservative establishment. The provenance of the source—an SPD newspaper in early 1933—makes it valuable for showing how political opponents viewed the backstairs intrigue; the Social Democrats clearly saw the appointment as a corrupt conspiracy by the elites rather than a legitimate democratic victory. Contextually, this is accurate to the events of January 1933, where the \"Cabinet of Barons\" and wealthy backers like Hjalmar Schacht played a pivotal role in brokering the deal, making the source a highly useful reflection of the political dynamics of the time.</p>"
                    },
                    {
                        "question": "3b. Study Interpretations 1 and 2. What is the main difference between these views? (4 marks)",
                        "model": "The main difference is their focus on the primary cause of Hitler's appointment. Interpretation 1 focuses on long-term structural factors, arguing that Hitler became Chancellor because the Weimar democratic system had already collapsed due to the Great Depression and the overuse of Article 48. In contrast, Interpretation 2 focuses on short-term human actions, arguing that Hitler's rise was the direct result of \"backstairs intrigue\" and the deliberate, foolish plotting of conservative elites like von Papen and Hindenburg who thought they could control him."
                    },
                    {
                        "question": "3c. Suggest one reason why Interpretation 1 and 2 give different views about how Hitler became Chancellor. (4 marks)",
                        "model": "One reason for the difference is that the historians have relied on different types of sources. The author of Interpretation 1 likely focused on official government and economic records, such as Reichstag election results, unemployment statistics, and the frequency of Article 48 decrees, leading to a focus on systemic collapse. Meanwhile, the author of Interpretation 2 likely relied on personal sources, such as the diaries, memoirs, and private correspondence of key figures like von Papen and Hindenburg, leading them to emphasize the secret meetings and personal conspiracies of the elite."
                    },
                    {
                        "question": "3d. How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about how Hitler became Chancellor? (16 marks)",
                        "model": "<p>Interpretation 2 presents a highly convincing argument that Hitler became Chancellor primarily due to the \"backstairs intrigue\" and the catastrophic miscalculations of the conservative elite. I strongly agree with this view regarding the immediate, short-term causes of the January 1933 appointment, although Interpretation 1 provides essential context regarding the structural collapse of Weimar democracy that made such intrigue possible.</p><p>Interpretation 2 is strongly supported by the historical evidence of the secret negotiations that took place in late 1932 and early 1933. As the interpretation notes, figures like Franz von Papen and President Hindenburg \"conspired to oust General von Schleicher.\" Papen, humiliated by his dismissal, met with Hitler at the home of banker Kurt von Schröder to broker a deal. This aligns perfectly with Source C, where Papen arrogantly boasts, \"We have hired him!\" and claims they have Hitler \"framed in.\" Papen's belief that he could control Hitler like a \"puppet\"—a view vividly corroborated by the SPD cartoon in Source D—was the decisive factor that convinced Hindenburg to offer Hitler the Chancellorship. Hindenburg despised Hitler, referring to him as the \"Bohemian corporal,\" and would never have appointed him without Papen's assurance that the conservative majority in the cabinet would keep the Nazis in check. Therefore, Interpretation 2 is highly accurate in identifying elite conspiracy as the direct mechanism of Hitler's rise.</p><p>However, Interpretation 1 offers a vital counter-argument by highlighting that this elite intrigue did not happen in a vacuum; it was necessitated by the \"systemic collapse of the Weimar political structure.\" I agree with Interpretation 1 that the Great Depression had \"paralyzed the moderate parties.\" By 1932, Chancellors like Brüning and Papen were ruling almost entirely via Article 48, bypassing the Reichstag. Democracy was indeed \"already dead.\" The elites only turned to Hitler because they desperately needed his mass support to legitimize their authoritarian rule and crush the Communists. The Nazis were the largest party in the Reichstag (winning 230 seats in July 1932), making them impossible to ignore. If the democratic system had been functioning healthily, the backroom deals described in Interpretation 2 would not have been necessary or possible.</p><p>In conclusion, I agree with Interpretation 2 to a large extent. While Interpretation 1 correctly identifies the underlying conditions—the economic misery of the Depression and the paralysis of the Reichstag—these factors alone did not guarantee Hitler the Chancellorship. In fact, Nazi vote share had actually fallen in November 1932, and the party was facing bankruptcy. It was the specific, deliberate, and foolish actions of von Papen and Hindenburg, as emphasized in Interpretation 2 and supported by Sources C and D, that rescued Hitler from political decline and handed him the keys to power. Therefore, elite intrigue was the definitive final step in Hitler becoming Chancellor.</p>"
                    }
